      "text": "To find the total surface area of the right pyramid, we need to calculate the area of the base and the slant heights of the lateral faces. The base is a square with side length 10 cm, so its area is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Base Area} = 10 \\times 10 = 100 \\text{ cm}^2 \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the slant height of the lateral faces. The slant height is the height of the triangular face from the apex to the midpoint of one of the sides of the base. We can use the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by the height of the pyramid (12 cm), half the side length of the base (5 cm), and the slant height.\n\nLet \\( l \\) be the slant height. Then:\n\n\\[ l = \\sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} = \\sqrt{144 + 25} = \\sqrt{169} = 13 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the area of one of the triangular lateral faces. The area of a triangle is given by:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area of one triangular face} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 13 = 65 \\text{ cm}^2 \\]\n\nSince there are four triangular faces, the total area of the lateral faces is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Lateral Surface Area} = 4 \\times 65 = 260 \\text{ cm}^2 \\]\n\nFinally, the total surface area of the pyramid is the sum of the area of the base and the lateral surface area:\n\n\\[ \\text{Total Surface Area} = 100 + 260 = 360 \\text{ cm}^2 \\]\n\nThus, the total surface area of the pyramid is \\(\\boxed{360}\\).",
